WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:21.398
Meanwhile Javert was frustrated that he had missed his chance to recapture Jean Valjean.

2
00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:27.221
Like everyone else, Javert had believed that Valjean had died falling from the ship.

3
00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:34.008
But when he read about the kidnapping of a girl named Cosette, Javert remembered that name.

4
00:00:34.949 --> 00:00:37.910
The newspaper also mentioned that her mother was Fantine.

5
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.930
Javert decided to visit the Thenardiers.

6
00:00:41.971 --> 00:00:44.231
Monsieur Thenardier was not cooperative.

7
00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:48.273
At first he had been outraged when Valjean removed Cosette.

8
00:00:49.173 --> 00:00:52.194
But then he realized there could be trouble if he informed the police.

9
00:00:53.314 --> 00:00:58.515
They might want to know where he had gotten fifteen hundred francs, or they might question some of

10
00:00:58.556 --> 00:00:59.876
his business dealings.

11
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.085
So when Javert came nosing around, Thenardier told him that Cosette had left with her grandfather.

12
00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:14.132
Javert was satisfied until he began hearing tales of "the generous beggar" whose description seemed to match Valjean's.

13
00:01:15.553 --> 00:01:22.239
He borrowed the clothes of another beggar, and soon discovered that his suspicions were correct—"the generous beggar" was

14
00:01:22.379 --> 00:01:23.500
indeed Valjean!

15
00:01:24.561 --> 00:01:28.164
Javert chased Valjean, but lost his prey in the cul-de-sac.

16
00:01:29.012 --> 00:01:35.437
For a month after Valjean's escape, Javert watched the neighborhood surrounding the old Gorbeau house, but he finally

17
00:01:35.497 --> 00:01:35.918
gave up.

18
00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:41.182
Years passed while Valjean was happily settled at the convent with Cosette.

19
00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:44.044
New tenants came to live at the Gorbeau house.

20
00:01:44.965 --> 00:01:49.629
They were the Jondrette family and a young man named Marius Pontmercy.

21
00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:57.915
The Jondrettes were extremely poor, and all four of them—mother, father, and two nearly grown daughters—lived in the

22
00:01:57.955 --> 00:01:58.536
same room.

23
00:01:59.432 --> 00:02:03.155
There was also a son named Gavroche, but he lived in the streets.

24
00:02:03.735 --> 00:02:07.678
His mother adored her daughters but cared nothing about Gavroche.

25
00:02:08.438 --> 00:02:15.563
The Jondrettes had formerly lived in Montfermeil, where they managed an inn and went by the name Thenardier.

26
00:02:17.144 --> 00:02:22.348
As for Marius, he had a connection with the Jondrettes, but he didn't recognize it yet.

27
00:02:23.468 --> 00:02:28.512
And now in order to go forward with our story, we must go back into the past.

28
00:02:29.796 --> 00:02:35.578
Readers may remember that Monsieur Thenardier claimed to have been a soldier in Napoleon's army and to have

29
00:02:35.638 --> 00:02:36.538
rescued a general.

30
00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:40.099
He even painted that scene on his inn's sign.

31
00:02:40.679 --> 00:02:42.880
In truth, he had never been a soldier.

32
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.121
After the Battle of Waterloo, all he did was loot the bodies of the dead!

33
00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:53.963
But one officer—a colonel, not a general—was still alive.

34
00:02:55.023 --> 00:02:59.104
As he went through the man's pockets, Thenardier accidentally revived him.

35
00:03:00.032 --> 00:03:06.755
The officer, whose name was Pontmercy, tried to reward Thenardier with his wallet and watch, but could not

36
00:03:06.855 --> 00:03:07.395
find them.

37
00:03:08.536 --> 00:03:12.617
Of course, he did not realize that Thenardier had taken the wallet and watch!

38
00:03:13.498 --> 00:03:19.680
Pontmercy vowed that he would someday find a way to repay Thenardier for saving his life.

39
00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:24.582
After the defeat of Napoleon, the monarchy was restored in France.

40
00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:29.064
Colonel Pontmercy went to live in the town of Vernon.

41
00:03:29.724 --> 00:03:34.406
As a supporter of Napoleon, Pontmercy was treated with constant suspicion.

42
00:03:35.327 --> 00:03:37.668
But this was not the worst of his problems.

43
00:03:38.288 --> 00:03:42.130
His wife died, leaving him with a young son named Marius.

44
00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:48.273
And the colonel's father-in-law, Monsieur Gillenormand, insisted on taking the boy.

45
00:03:48.293 --> 00:03:56.688
"If you ever attempt to see Marius, I'll disinherit him," threatened Gillenormand.

46
00:03:56.708 --> 00:04:00.951
Since there was a lot of money at stake, Pontmercy did as he was told.

47
00:04:01.531 --> 00:04:03.312
But he missed his son terribly.

48
00:04:04.272 --> 00:04:07.214
Gillenormand lived with his unmarried daughter in Paris.

49
00:04:07.935 --> 00:04:13.518
Every few months Pontmercy traveled to Paris and went to the Church of Saint Sulpice.

50
00:04:14.578 --> 00:04:19.261
There he sat behind a pillar where he could secretly observe Marius during the service.

51
00:04:20.342 --> 00:04:24.044
Twice a year Aunt Gillenormand dictated letters to Marius.

52
00:04:24.808 --> 00:04:28.049
The boy dutifully wrote them down and sent them to his father.

53
00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:36.151
Pontmercy responded with long, tender letters, but Monsieur Gillenormand never showed them to his grandson.

54
00:04:37.072 --> 00:04:40.053
So Marius grew up with little love for his father.

55
00:04:40.573 --> 00:04:44.754
To complicate matters, he and his father were on opposite sides politically.

56
00:04:45.554 --> 00:04:51.136
Marius had been raised with the same beliefs as his grandfather, who was a supporter of the monarchy.

57
00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.981
His father remained a supporter of the fallen emperor.

58
00:04:55.821 --> 00:05:01.043
By the time Cosette and Valjean were at the convent, Marius had become a young man.

59
00:05:02.143 --> 00:05:09.345
One day his grandfather said, "Marius, tomorrow you will go to Vernon to see your father.

60
00:05:10.066 --> 00:05:12.587
He is ill and asking for you."

61
00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:16.988
Marius was so shocked, he didn't question his grandfather.

62
00:05:17.568 --> 00:05:22.651
The next night he arrived at Vernon only to find that his father had just died.

63
00:05:23.631 --> 00:05:28.734
Marius remained in Vernon for two days, pretending to mourn the father he hadn't known.

64
00:05:29.635 --> 00:05:33.457
The sale of Colonel Pontmercy's furniture barely paid for his funeral.

65
00:05:35.117 --> 00:05:40.520
He hadn't left a will, only a scrap of paper, which a servant handed to Marius.

66
00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:46.984
"For my son: Emperor Napoleon made me a baron at the Battle of Waterloo.

67
00:05:47.844 --> 00:05:52.408
I hand that title down to you, knowing that you will bear it proudly."

68
00:05:53.549 --> 00:05:58.653
On the back the colonel had added: "At this same battle, a man saved my life.

69
00:05:59.754 --> 00:06:04.038
His name is Thenardier, and he's an innkeeper at Montfermeil.

70
00:06:04.659 --> 00:06:08.222
If you ever meet him, please do whatever you can for him."

71
00:06:09.323 --> 00:06:12.045
Marius took the paper and folded it in his pocket.

72
00:06:12.625 --> 00:06:17.957
He went back to Paris, where he was studying law, and thought no more of his father.

73
00:06:18.897 --> 00:06:24.780
The young man had kept up the religious habits of his childhood, often attending church at Saint Sulpice.

74
00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.802
One Sunday he was kneeling in prayer when an old man interrupted him.

75
00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:34.884
"Monsieur, you're sitting in my pew," said the old man.

76
00:06:36.064 --> 00:06:38.645
Marius moved away and the old man sat down.

77
00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:41.386
After mass the old man approached him.

78
00:06:41.406 --> 00:06:49.305
"You probably think me rude for asking you to move," said the old man, introducing himself as Monsieur

79
00:06:49.385 --> 00:06:52.547
Mabeuf. "I must explain and beg your pardon."

80
00:06:52.567 --> 00:06:57.369
"That's entirely unnecessary, monsieur," replied Marius.

81
00:06:58.369 --> 00:07:00.890
But Mabeuf insisted and went on with his story.

82
00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:04.872
"I always feel that mass is better from that seat.

83
00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:11.474
For many years I watched a father sit in that pew and silently observe his son.

84
00:07:12.035 --> 00:07:15.316
The unfortunate man had no other way to see his child.

85
00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.761
He always hid behind the pillar, so the boy wouldn't see him.

86
00:07:20.921 --> 00:07:27.203
I could tell that the man worshipped his son, but the boy probably didn't even know he existed."

87
00:07:28.363 --> 00:07:29.504
Marius was puzzled.

88
00:07:29.524 --> 00:07:32.265
"Why wouldn't the boy know his father?"

89
00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:40.207
"The father had a rich relative who threatened to disinherit the boy if the father tried to see

90
00:07:40.287 --> 00:07:47.381
him. The father had served under Napoleon; he and his father-in-law were on opposite sides politically.

91
00:07:48.281 --> 00:07:56.763
The father lived in Vernon and his name was something like Pontmarie or Montpercy."

92
00:07:56.783 --> 00:08:00.825
"Pontmercy," said Marius, turning pale.

93
00:08:00.845 --> 00:08:03.685
"Exactly! Did you know him?"

94
00:08:03.705 --> 00:08:07.286
"Monsieur, he was my father."

95
00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:10.427
The old man clasped Marius' hands.

96
00:08:10.447 --> 00:08:13.528
"So you were the child!

97
00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:19.986
I'm sorry that you never knew him, but you can say that you had a father who loved

98
00:08:20.046 --> 00:08:20.646
you well."

99
00:08:21.727 --> 00:08:25.671
From that moment on, Marius learned all he could about his father.

100
00:08:26.211 --> 00:08:30.115
He came to respect the man and to share his political views.

101
00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:38.783
Marius had calling cards printed up with his title, Baron Marius Pontmercy, and wore his father's "will" in

102
00:08:38.823 --> 00:08:39.304
a locket.

103
00:08:40.585 --> 00:08:45.171
One day Marius left home in a hurry, leaving behind the cards and locket.

104
00:08:45.852 --> 00:08:48.116
His grandfather found them.
